#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# root_approximant

#% text
# `root_approximant(x)` gives an algebraic number of degree at most 8 and small coefficients that is close to the number x.

#% text
# `root_approximant(x, n)` allows degree at most n.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For each degree d the integer relation search (PSLQ) is run on 1, x, ..., x^d; the first polynomial whose coefficients are small compared with the precision of x is taken, and the result is its root nearest x, written as `solve` writes it.

#% item
# With n given and no small polynomial found, the polynomial of degree n is taken.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A square root:

#% cell
root_approximant(n(sqrt(67)))
#% output
#= 67^(1/2)

#% text
# A degree-4 number:

#% cell
root_approximant(n(sqrt(2) + sqrt(3)), 4)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2) + 3^(1/2)

#% text
# An exact number is evaluated first:

#% cell
root_approximant(sqrt(2 + sqrt(3)))
#% output
#= (1/2)*(2^(1/2) + 6^(1/2))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `find_integer_null_vector`, `root_reduce`, `minimal_polynomial`, `rationalize`
